::  name       comlink chunked carriage bound
::  shape      beads -- int (@u32), bound 8 -- the most pieces a whole is allowed to divide into
::  invariant  a whole this tree accepts always divides into the pieces it allows, and the compiler says so
::  example    8
::  readers    Comlink - Mantra - Amphora - Glow Tend - shape museum
::  nib        comlink-carriage-v1 at 20260830.200925
::
::  Comlink's third pedestal, and the second of its bounds. The first desk named the number a
::  single datagram leaves for its message -- 340 bytes. This one names what happens when the
::  thing being carried is larger than that: it is cut into pieces, and the pieces are counted.
::
::  THE RELATION, IN ONE LINE. Three families in this tree cut a whole into pieces and carry
::  them. Each publishes three numbers -- the whole's ceiling, one piece's size, and the most
::  pieces allowed -- and for a whole the module ACCEPTS to actually cross, one relation must
::  hold:
::
::    whole <= pieces * piece_size
::
::  Break it and nothing crashes and nothing corrupts. A caller hands over a whole the module
::  accepted at its own door, and three lines later the same module refuses it -- TooManyBeads,
::  or Overflow -- for being too large to carry. A legal input, refused by the code that called
::  it legal.
::
::  THE THREE FAMILIES, MEASURED ON METAL 20260830 by building them and printing the constants:
::
::    family  rooms                                          whole  piece  pieces  capacity  slack
::    batch   mantra/recall_batch_wire + mantra/resin_batch    4096    331      16      5296   1200
::    vessel  amphora/vessel_fetch_wire                        1024    267       8      2136   1112
::    bead    mantra/beading + mantra/recall_lap1               512     64       8       512      0
::
::  WHY THE BEAD ROW IS WHY THIS DESK EXISTS. Its slack is zero. Not small -- zero. A 512-byte
::  resin is the largest this tree accepts, and the content-defined chunker in
::  mantra/beading.rye cuts no piece shorter than `cdc_min_bead`, which is 64. Eight pieces of
::  sixty-four is five hundred and twelve exactly, and `max_beads` is eight. The fence post
::  stands precisely on the line.
::
::  Both of the numbers holding it there are the kind a routine lap moves. `cdc_min_bead` sits
::  among the chunker's TUNING constants, beside a hash mask and a comment about average piece
::  size -- filed under how the cutting behaves rather than under how much fits. And
::  `max_resin_bytes` is a capacity that three rooms spell and a growing tree naturally raises.
::
::  MEASURED, RATHER THAN REASONED. Feeding 3,999 pseudorandom wholes through
::  `bead_content_defined` on metal:
::
::    as it stands, whole=512:  0 refused, the worst content reaching exactly 8 beads
::    max_resin_bytes 512->576: 9 refused with TooManyBeads, roughly one in every 444
::
::  Nine in four thousand is the shape of the harm. It is not a failure a sample finds, because
::  it depends on the bytes: the same edit passes for 3,990 wholes and refuses for nine. A test
::  written with one fixture would have shipped it, and the failure would have arrived later, in
::  production, on somebody's actual content.
::
::  WHY A DESK, RATHER THAN A COMMENT. The relation spans two rooms in every family. The batch
::  family's whole is `rb.max_batch_bytes` in mantra/resin_batch.rye and its carriage bound is
::  `max_batch_chunks` in mantra/recall_batch_wire.rye, and each file reads correct alone. That
::  is the whole difficulty: no reader of either file is looking at the number that would make
::  the other one wrong.
::
::  WHAT IS WIRED, AND WHAT IS ONLY WRITTEN. From 20260830 the two families this lane holds
::  carry the relation at comptime, so the COMPILER refuses an edit that breaks it:
::
::    mantra/beading.rye            three ties -- the content-defined path, the fixed-size path,
::                                  and the claim its own max_beads comment has made since
::                                  20260706, that a full bead-index is itself a legal resin
::    mantra/recall_batch_wire.rye  one tie, reaching across into resin_batch's ceiling
::
::  Every one was proven able to REFUSE before it shipped -- inverted, and then by the plausible
::  real edits: `cdc_min_bead` lowered to 32 stops the build, `max_resin_bytes` raised to 576
::  stops the build, `max_batch_chunks` halved stops the build. A tie proven only in the passing
::  direction cannot be told from a comptime block nobody evaluates.
::
::  THREE READINGS CARRY THIS DESK, and each can fail while the other two agree. The trios the
::  desk lists equal what the Rye publishes, so a bound moved in one room and not the desk is
::  seen. The relation holds for every trio by the scan's own arithmetic, computed rather than
::  pinned, so an honest raise in both rooms walks free. And the comptime ties still stand in the
::  rooms that hold them -- delete those blocks and the first two readings agree perfectly over
::  code nothing proves will carry what it accepts.
::
::  THE SLACK IS REPORTED, NEVER GATED. A lap that gives beading real headroom -- raising
::  `cdc_min_bead` to 128, say -- moves the tightest slack from 0 to 512, and that is better work
::  rather than a fault. A guard that reds on an improvement is a guard someone turns off.
::
::  WHAT THIS DESK DOES NOT REACH. Whether these are the right ceilings, which is Comlink's and
::  Mantra's own design question; this room reads them. Whether a whole SHOULD be cut at all, and
::  at what size, which is the chunker's business. And amphora/vessel_fetch_wire.rye, which
::  carries the third family in the same shape with 1,112 bytes of slack: that room is Amphora's
::  to tie, and it is read and displayed here rather than edited.
+$  comlink-chunked-carriage-bound-shape
  $:  beads=@u32
  ==
